This is Jack Van Horn from the University
of Southern California and the Big Data to

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.480
Knowledge Training Coordinating Center.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.210
And I'd like to welcome you to this.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:12.070
Our final, in our series, of a guide to the
fundamentals of data science seminar series.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:18.261
Today, we're very delighted to have Dr. Patricia
Flatley Brennan, director of the National

00:01:18.261 --> 00:01:23.470
Library of Medicine and Interim Associate
NIH director for data science, speaking on

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:27.120
data science at the NIH current state and
future directions.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:34.690
We're very delighted to have Dr. Brennan,
who is the former Lillian L. Moehlman Bascom

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:39.320
Professor at the School of Nursing and College
of Engineering at the University of Wisconsin,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.320
Madison.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.600
Where she led the living environments laboratory
at the Wisconsin Institutes for Discovery.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.930
Developed Computer Link, an electronic network
designed to reduce isolation improved self-care

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.360
amongst home health care patients.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.080
And directed heart care, directed health design.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:06.080
She has a long list of accolades, and her
CV must be pages and pages and chapters long.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:15.000
In particular, most recently, she has been
the director of the National Library of Medicine

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.160
since August of 2016.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.540
And recently, as of January of 2017, took
on the role of the Interim Associate NIH director

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.239
for data science, the ads office that many
of you are familiar with.

00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:37.030
And as part of that, she is in a leadership
position for the BD2K enterprise, many of

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.240
which you participate in.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.480
So we're very delighted to get some insights
from her about the current state of data science

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:45.480
at the NIH.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:52.879
What future directions hold through data science
at the NIH for biomedical research, progress

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:57.060
in all manner of data mining, and computation,
and informatics, et cetera.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.310
So we're very delighted to have Dr. Brennan.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.280
And I would like to ask you, that if you have
any questions for Dr. Brennan, please use

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:10.560
the Go To webinar question submission feature,
and you can submit your questions, and about

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.550
10 minutes before the hour, we'll take those
questions.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.159
I'll read them off to her, and she'll get
a chance to answer.

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:21.090
So without further delay, Dr. Brennan, thank
you so much for joining us.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.569
Thanks very much and I appreciate the kind
introduction.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:26.780
I thank all of you for taking time out of
your day today.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.129
If it's as beautiful where you are as it is
here in Washington, you're missing a bright

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:38.999
sunny, but rather hot day, outside to participate
in this last of the big data science seminars,

00:03:38.999 --> 00:03:41.900
held now for almost for a year.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.099
I want to say how happy I am to be a part
of this seminar series, and how glad I am

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:47.629
to be at this point in time.

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:51.760
Certainly, there have been some notable speakers
that have gone ahead of me, bringing you both

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.749
technical solutions, I hope, and new perspectives
in thinking about data and data science.

00:03:57.749 --> 00:04:01.879
My role today is to accomplish four things
with you.

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:05.959
First, I want to give you an update on what's
going on at NIH with data science.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:06.959
There's many things.

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:09.939
It's a vibrant, active part of our mission.

00:04:09.939 --> 00:04:13.989
Second, I want to provide you with some of
my views and perspectives on data science,

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:18.910
so you can understand how I'm approaching
this challenge, and perhaps, guide me with

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.750
your ideas of how to make sure we create a
robust infrastructure for discovery.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:27.230
Third, I'm going to explain our near term
and at your

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:32.200
efforts, in both the intramural NIH campus
level investments, as well as our national

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.880
investments in data science.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.980
And finally-- and I hope to actually save
even more time than the 10 minutes at the

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:44.250
end-- to get your thoughts and ideas about
how the NIH can facilitate data science, data

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:49.370
driven discovery, and the clinical practice,
and health improvement of our country, based

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.830
on investments in data driven resources.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:56.870
I want you to think about what kind of services
would be needed in the future.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.320
What kind of supports do you need?

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.610
Are you looking forward to certain types of
granting programs, certain types of methodological

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:03.610
issues?

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.030
Should we be thinking about workforce development.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.860
Those kinds of resources I would very much
appreciate your guidance on.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:15.850
And I'm going to ask Crystal and the organizers
if they can record all of the comments that

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:21.070
come in, even if we don't get to address all
of them in our conversation today.

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:25.630
Let me begin by taking you to the end.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.220
Why do we actually want to invest in data
science?

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.350
We believe that fundamentally, data science
is a pathway to discovery, is a new and essential

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:40.280
pathway to discovery, and along that pathway
there are opportunities, there are challenges,

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.600
and there's a need for some direction.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:50.550
The idea that data drives discovery is a fundamental
shift, not simply in the methods we use, but

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.800
in the way we conduct research.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.830
And at NIH, this commitment is very strong,
and it begins at that highest level of leadership.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:03.010
Dr. Collins has personally invested in how
to create the proper infrastructure for data

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.010
science.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:10.980
The BD2K program, that was familiar to most
of you over the years, began as an inquiry

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:16.590
spurned on by the enormous amount of data
being generated through genomic and proteomic

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.830
studies in the late 90s and early 2000s.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:26.410
A realization that we needed to enable biomedical
sciences to capitalize on the data being generated

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:27.960
by research communities.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:35.180
And so the BD2K program launched after a series
of workshops held, that met with many different

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:41.290
sectors, many different stakeholders, and
came to focus on the idea that we needed to

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.190
provide training infrastructure.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:47.870
We needed to stimulate all types of research
in new areas about data.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:53.280
We needed to stimulate basic methodological
inquiry, as well as investment in the infrastructure

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:54.710
needed to support data.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.370
And what you see on the screen here is our
home page for data science at NIH.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:04.280
And I call your attention to the bar at the
bottom, which describes the data science community,

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.660
the big data to knowledge process, and the
comments.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:12.440
The data science community is broader than
anyone ever expected, bringing in, not only

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.810
genomic space researchers, but also people
with advanced analytics, machine learning,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.150
and artificial intelligence specialists.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:24.210
Even bringing in engineers and computer scientists
and designing new data models, new kinds of

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.150
resources, and storage strategies.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:31.890
In addition, the library sciences community
has played an increasingly large role as part

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:37.120
of the data science community with their skills
and curation standards setting, dissemination,

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.000
and collection management.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.750
We organize a lot of the thinking around data
science, under the concept of a commons, and

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.580
we'll return to this
phrase several times, I know you've had other

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:50.680
discussions about it earlier throughout this
year.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:55.920
From the perspective of the NIH, at this point
in time, is that commons is both a concept,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.860
as well as a physical space.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:02.210
And we'll be talking about some of the work
that's being done to set up the NIH commons.

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:09.520
The idea is the commons provides a safe harbor
for data to be stored, but also a pathway

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.700
for data to be accessed and used.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:18.040
We envision that data commons is not only
serving a repository for data, but also as

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:24.451
a suite of services, resources for modeling,
for curation, and for establishing a good

00:08:24.451 --> 00:08:27.080
catalog of data and information.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:31.050
Let me speak to specifically about the BD2K
program right now, for that's what brought

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.709
a lot of people into this process.

00:08:33.709 --> 00:08:41.700
As you know, the BD2K program was intended
to be about $125-$130 million initiative per

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:44.180
year, over about an eight year period.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:46.700
And we're about halfway through this period.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:52.110
At this time, with the departure of Phil Bourne
and the reorganization of the associate director

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.920
of data science office, we've had many discussions
with the NIH leadership about how to make

00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:03.720
sure we're getting the yield and value out
of the NIH investment in the Big Data to Knowledge

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:07.079
Project.

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:12.870
We anticipate that this project will continue
from now through its intended target date

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:14.180
of 2021.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.510
And the project is fully funded through this
period of time.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.450
And yet, there are changes that are being
made.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:26.180
Those changes are summarized in a blog post
that we provided on February the 23rd.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:31.009
The link to the blog post is on the screen
in front of you.

00:09:31.009 --> 00:09:34.480
But essentially, let me summarize what's in
that blog post.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:41.670
First we have the BD2K program resources were
designed to provide extramural support, that

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:47.420
is out of NIH support, for data discovery,
data curation, data management.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:53.100
We remain committed to all existing programs,
and so they will be funded at there originally

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.100
intended level.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:59.290
All programs that are active-- this was as
of January, but continues through now-- all

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:05.701
programs that are active will be funded through
the end point of their period of time, many

00:10:05.701 --> 00:10:10.149
of them winding up by August of 2018.

00:10:10.149 --> 00:10:17.160
We added one new program funding this spring,
and that was an initiative on curation.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:25.110
That had been released prior to the planned
pivoting of the BD2K program, and that was

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:30.010
that the announcements about the funding of
those new projects will come out during this

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.010
summer.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:35.199
They are specifically targeting innovations
in curation, including some very exciting

00:10:35.199 --> 00:10:40.540
uses of natural language processing to facilitate
automated curation.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:46.680
We are also, at this time, considering and
putting together the think tank within NIH

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:53.200
to plan for future needs, including training,
research, infrastructure, and policy development.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:58.199
What's important about this is during the
process of pivoting from the extramural program,

00:10:58.199 --> 00:11:03.980
to the next phase of BD2K, we recognize that
we have a rapidly growing amount of data that

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:06.380
still lacks a safe place for storage.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:12.949
So during this two year period of time, we
are investing more in creating NIH based resources

00:11:12.949 --> 00:11:18.399
for our high value data sets and for access
for researchers to begin to compute on those

00:11:18.399 --> 00:11:19.399
data sets.

00:11:19.399 --> 00:11:23.561
I'll return to that those comments in a few
minutes, after we talk about, in

00:11:23.561 --> 00:11:26.660
general, the process of discovery through
data.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:31.220
But remember, I'm hoping that you will continue
to jot down your thoughts, and think about

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:36.610
how the NIH can foster data driven discovery
as you perceive it from your own workplace

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.740
and your own work career.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:43.089
But let's pause for a moment and think about
where discoveries originate from.

00:11:43.089 --> 00:11:49.470
And why the NIH should be investing in creating
an infrastructure for discovery.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:54.910
Early discoveries of the health sciences were
originated through the process of experience.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:58.990
An individual, a clinician, a healer, worked
with the patient, worked with a person in

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:01.920
need, made observations about what happened.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:06.439
The response, hopefully an improvement, because
of that additional sunshine, certain herbs,

00:12:06.439 --> 00:12:09.069
or maybe certain kinds of medication.

00:12:09.069 --> 00:12:14.939
These processes of experience were translated
to written documents, and the pathway for

00:12:14.939 --> 00:12:21.050
discovery became a one time experience with
an individual shared in a narrative or heuristic

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:22.050
fashion.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:29.620
As we move to the late 18th, early 19th century,
we began to see the embracing of experimental

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:32.910
design as a pathway to discovery.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:39.079
Experimental design had lots of advantages
that allowed us to focus specifically on phenomenon,

00:12:39.079 --> 00:12:43.779
to maybe discern the pathway that explain
why certain phenomenon occurred in a certain

00:12:43.779 --> 00:12:45.600
fashion or did not occur.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.140
It let us determine which techniques work
better than others.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:54.899
Increasingly, experiments also brought about
a perspective on science that allowed for

00:12:54.899 --> 00:13:01.749
excluding, or controlling for, variability
in the process that was being observed by

00:13:01.749 --> 00:13:07.530
ensuring that careful procedures were followed,
by ensuring that certain variables, certain

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:12.160
aspects of the phenomenon, were highlighted,
and others were not attended to.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:18.050
And precision in measurement and understanding
allowed for a process of discovery.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:23.450
This philosophy was a very-- what I would
call-- particularistic approach to discovery,

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:29.329
that essentially required the investigator
to design a priori, an experiment that had

00:13:29.329 --> 00:13:34.079
the appropriate variables, the appropriate
manipulations, and the appropriate controls,

00:13:34.079 --> 00:13:39.029
and allowing and learning through prior studies
for example, or through study of the literature.

00:13:39.029 --> 00:13:44.370
What aspects of the phenomenon needed to be
attended to, and what could be ignored?

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:47.690
Part of the value of experimentation was its
narrowness of focus.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:50.779
But part of the limitation was its narrowness
of focus.

00:13:50.779 --> 00:13:55.160
And increasingly, over time, experiments generated
more and more data.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:01.200
As we moved into the 60s, in the US and around
the world, computation became an important

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:04.490
complement to the experimental design.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:08.360
Computation now freed us from being too overly
restrictive on the number of variables that

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:11.519
could be observed and analyzed at the same
time.

00:14:11.519 --> 00:14:16.759
And allowed for an increasing augmentation,
not only of the research process, but in the

00:14:16.759 --> 00:14:22.029
cognition of the individual, who was trying
to understand phenomenon and plot the pathway

00:14:22.029 --> 00:14:23.249
through.

00:14:23.249 --> 00:14:28.449
We now are moving into, what I think of as,
the fourth generation of discovery processes.

00:14:28.449 --> 00:14:30.899
And that is data driven discovery.

00:14:30.899 --> 00:14:36.129
Here we are freed from the constraints that
experiments once required us to adhere to.

00:14:36.129 --> 00:14:38.690
That is a
limitation in the number of variables, or

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:43.509
limitation in the consideration of what aspects
needs to be considered.

00:14:43.509 --> 00:14:50.790
And it brings us to the ability to understand
phenomenon in context, in much greater detail.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:57.510
The challenge of managing this information,
as a process of discovery, is going to require

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:03.529
changes in the way we think about the substrate,
the infrastructure, to support discovery.

00:15:03.529 --> 00:15:07.449
When we think in a data driven era of discovery,
we're envisioning data as a substrate that

00:15:07.449 --> 00:15:14.060
is both generated by experiments and observations,
and then becomes subsequently, later on, the

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:17.259
substance that future discoveries are rest
on.

00:15:17.259 --> 00:15:22.959
So we see a process here of accelerating discovery,
activities conducted once, can generate data

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:25.869
that can be re-used many times.

00:15:25.869 --> 00:15:32.379
Fundamentally, data driven discovery rests
on two aspects: data and computation.

00:15:32.379 --> 00:15:37.029
I'm not going to go into too much depth about
the fair principles of data.

00:15:37.029 --> 00:15:40.089
I'm sure they're well familiar to the audience
that we're speaking.

00:15:40.089 --> 00:15:44.490
But for those of you who may be less familiar
with it, let me just say, straight forward,

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:50.589
that there is a acceptance around the world
that for data driven discovery, we must have

00:15:50.589 --> 00:15:57.899
a quality of data that makes sure that those
data are findable, are accessible, are interoperable,

00:15:57.899 --> 00:15:59.329
and reusable.

00:15:59.329 --> 00:16:04.779
For data to be findable, we need to attend
to the labeling, the metadata, the curation

00:16:04.779 --> 00:16:10.890
of the data to ensure it is locatable through
machine interaction.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:14.470
That begins with making sure the data are
well labeled and cataloged and described in

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:16.490
a way that can be discovered.

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:21.499
Ensuring that data are accessible is not only
a technical challenge, but it's also a challenge

00:16:21.499 --> 00:16:28.959
of ensuring that the authentication and authorization
to use data sets, is appropriately managed,

00:16:28.959 --> 00:16:34.050
so that data are managed in a respectable
fashion.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:39.769
Interoperability is that characteristic that
we trust will lead us to be able to link data

00:16:39.769 --> 00:16:46.259
sets from more than one environment, from
more than one experiment, across several experiments.

00:16:46.259 --> 00:16:51.899
The interoperability of data requires that
data be findable, that his terms be used in

00:16:51.899 --> 00:16:54.329
a similar and consistent fashion.

00:16:54.329 --> 00:17:01.179
But also that they be accessible through platforms
that allow the transmission of analytics,

00:17:01.179 --> 00:17:04.550
as well as the transmission of the data themselves.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:09.530
Fundamentally, in data driven discovery, the
principle of reusability governs what we do.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:15.990
That is, we start from an original experiment
moving onto a recognition that the data generated

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:22.850
from one experiment, needs to be placed in
a storage strategy that is accessible to others.

00:17:22.850 --> 00:17:29.390
But reusability, like our other three characteristics
of data, is not simply a technical challenge,

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:31.610
but its also a social challenge.

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:38.760
Reuseable data, and discovery driven by reuseable
data sets, is a shift in the way research

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.679
is done and valued.

00:17:40.679 --> 00:17:45.559
Primary investigation often was considered
the hallmark of the best practices of research,

00:17:45.559 --> 00:17:52.300
and secondary analysis, while useful, was
not often awarded the same level of respect

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.830
and consideration, in terms of its value.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:59.840
Increasingly, we are now realizing, that one
single experiment-- even one that is designed

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:05.409
in a very complex fashion, engaging a lot
of investigators-- still doesn't yield all

00:18:05.409 --> 00:18:08.760
of the findings that the data set generated
in the

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:09.789
experiment could yield.

00:18:09.789 --> 00:18:14.860
And so we now need to think, not only how
to make that data reusable, but how to make

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:20.360
reusable data driven discovery approaches
to research, as valued, as useful for society

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:23.649
as our experimental models.

00:18:23.649 --> 00:18:25.330
Computational challenges remain.

00:18:25.330 --> 00:18:31.041
Data science is not simply statistics on steroids,
or bioinformatics with large groups, large

00:18:31.041 --> 00:18:32.280
sets of data.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:36.779
But really requires us to think about ways
we can bring in new kinds of analytics.

00:18:36.779 --> 00:18:41.360
One of the values of data driven discovery,
is that we are freed of the assumptions that

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:46.320
must be met, in order to be able to use classic
statistics in [INAUDIBLE] models.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:51.760
We are able now, to take data in its natural
form, and use that data in any way to help

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:55.620
discover by considering different approaches
to analytics.

00:18:55.620 --> 00:19:01.440
And I call your attention to developments
in three areas that I find particularly promising.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:06.049
Distributed analytics, machine learning, and
an optimization.

00:19:06.049 --> 00:19:10.340
Distributed analytics allow us to essentially
bring analytic tools to where the data reside

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:16.039
and return the results of an analysis to be
compiled and integrated in the fashion.

00:19:16.039 --> 00:19:21.110
This is a marked departure from our usual
multisite clinical trials models, where data

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:28.519
are collected in one place and gathered in
a central place, for subsequent analysis.

00:19:28.519 --> 00:19:33.170
Distributed analytics have a power to be able
to look at different phenomenon over time,

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:38.649
but importantly, also have the promise of
ensuring privacy preservation, so the data

00:19:38.649 --> 00:19:43.169
themselves are not transported, only the analytics
from the data.

00:19:43.169 --> 00:19:47.820
Machine learning strategies, particularly
deep learning, bring a chance to understand

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:55.120
data from its basic discovery model, and then
require a interpretation based on the frameworks

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.850
and the theories that are brought by domain
science.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:03.330
The values of machine learning is that it
lies in the fact that the patterning that

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:09.289
is assessed, is not constrained by certain
underlying assumptions of the data, but rather

00:20:09.289 --> 00:20:13.270
can be brought forward based on the existence
of the data itself.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:18.460
Essentially, we have a semantic free discovery
strategy, that later requires interpretation.

00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:20.789
This, by way, is also one of the downfalls.

00:20:20.789 --> 00:20:25.340
Many machine learning results are difficult
to explain to domain scientists or clinicians,

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:30.019
and therefore, require a longer pathway to
explanation.

00:20:30.019 --> 00:20:35.390
Optimization is an approach brought in from
operations research that allows us to look

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:39.960
at pathways through data sets, discovering
from the millions of possible trajectories,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:43.370
for example, or the millions of possible samples,
that could be drawn.

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:45.610
Which are the ones that would be most promising?

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:50.929
Optimization gives us a chance to approach
data in a more principled fashion, so that

00:20:50.929 --> 00:20:56.750
we can make the best use of the analytic powers
available for the data scientists discovery.

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:03.770
I see particular promise and visualization
strategies, and to a particular visual analytics,

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:08.389
the use of analytical display and the use
of visual displays of data, as an assistance

00:21:08.389 --> 00:21:12.730
in the process of analysis, and also in depicting
results.

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:17.169
One of the challenges of data science is that,
while it's not restricted to very large data

00:21:17.169 --> 00:21:22.539
sets, it's often employed in the analysis
of very large data sets, ones that are not

00:21:22.539 --> 00:21:26.680
human readable, or too large for a human to
comprehend.

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:32.070
And so the visualization tools allow us the
opportunities to explore these data sets and

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:36.010
examine and bring in human judgment as needed.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:39.630
It's important to spend a little bit of time
talking about some of the computational challenges

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:41.470
for the management of data.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:46.480
We have a great need to put in place, good
business processes, that allow for access,

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:53.649
access control, management, and the attempt
to preserve the proper alignment of the permission

00:21:53.649 --> 00:21:57.230
to use the data with the individual choosing
to use it.

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:02.640
Business management processes are also needed
to help us to anticipate the value of data

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:07.510
set, and the potential cost of curation, and
the cost of reuse of data sets, so we can

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:10.510
put proper pricing strategies in place.

00:22:10.510 --> 00:22:16.020
Another management challenges that we face,
is the preservation of the provenance of analytical

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.020
strategies.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:20.419
The enormous size of data sets and the teams
working with them, often leads us to having

00:22:20.419 --> 00:22:26.860
a very confused pipeline and the increasing
reliance on pipeline approaches to maintaining

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:32.690
the history of the analysis done with a particular
data set, will enable us to discover it in

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:35.149
a more reliable fashion.

00:22:35.149 --> 00:22:40.630
Finally, maintaining version control of the
data set, I believe presents a particular

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:43.940
challenge, that I think the National Library
of Medicine may have some particular skills

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:45.110
to solve.

00:22:45.110 --> 00:22:49.380
One of the things that we realized, is once
data sets are acquired, new investigators

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:54.390
bring new enrichment strategies to them, and
require, and make modifications that may be

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.470
well understood by that individual.

00:22:56.470 --> 00:23:02.210
This brings to us in need for managing version
control, for having the proper tools in place,

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:07.060
so that a basic data set remains intact, but
the enhancements and enrichments done by other

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:13.850
investigators, also remain accessible to the
community as it emerges.

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:17.610
I'd like to talk to now, about where I see
the National Library of Medicine fitting into

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:18.610
this process.

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:23.890
About three years ago, at NIH, a study was
done reported to the director of NIH that

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:28.429
said, that the National Library of Medicine
should become the epicenter of data science

00:23:28.429 --> 00:23:29.820
for the NIH.

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:32.630
Now epicenter has sort of a bad meeting, in
some places.

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:36.790
The center of an earthquake, or the center
of destruction, and I certainly hope the National

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:40.289
Library of Medicine does not become the center
of destruction.

00:23:40.289 --> 00:23:45.510
But I do believe with our long history, over
almost 200 years, of support for discovery

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:49.750
in our country and around the world, the National
Library of Medicine will play an important

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.899
role in accelerating data science.

00:23:51.899 --> 00:23:56.809
Let me show you a brief video that will introduce
you to some of the products and services at

00:23:56.809 --> 00:24:00.220
the National Library of Medicine currently
is involved with.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:44.299
And then we'll talk about how we're going
to be participating in the data driven discoveries

00:24:44.299 --> 00:24:55.880
of the future.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:25:18.919
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:25:18.919 --> 00:25:32.559
[END PLAYBACK]
This video introduced you to some of our treasures

00:25:32.559 --> 00:25:33.830
and some of our products.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:38.309
I'm sure you're well familiar with PubMed,
many of you probably also use dbGaP.

00:25:38.309 --> 00:25:45.159
Our PubMed, or citation database, has over
26 and 1/2 million citations to the biomedical

00:25:45.159 --> 00:25:47.750
literature, or to articles, around the world.

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:51.840
And adds almost 1 million citations every
year.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:57.059
Our resources are also though used by young
children and families around the world.

00:25:57.059 --> 00:25:58.259
You saw Medline Plus.

00:25:58.259 --> 00:26:04.139
You saw the [INAUDIBLE] young child, as he
heard the voice of a native healer, we preserve

00:26:04.139 --> 00:26:06.519
the healing traditions of the country.

00:26:06.519 --> 00:26:07.880
We also provide training.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:11.510
We also provide various ways to make sure
our resources are used.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:14.960
You might have been wondering why we shared
that slide of malaria and that cup of water

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:16.690
with the bugs in it.

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:22.710
We are trying to use our image processing
analysis to provide in the field detection

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:27.280
of different diseases, so that in under-resourced
country, we're able to take the knowledge

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:32.730
of the library into the decision making process
in the laboratory, as quickly as possible.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:39.909
So we do primarily support research, but our
resources, driven by the need to support research,

00:26:39.909 --> 00:26:41.200
are used globally.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:46.549
4 million times a day to PubMed Central, 300,000
times a day to dpGaP.

00:26:46.549 --> 00:26:48.680
Millions of individuals access our work.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:53.759
And you saw pathogen detection process that's
now used every day by public health authorities

00:26:53.759 --> 00:26:59.350
around the country, trying to determine the
exact nature of an E. coli or a listeria outbreak.

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:04.279
Essentially, our future bills and our passed,
and we are ready to take leadership in the

00:27:04.279 --> 00:27:06.090
area of data science around the country.

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:09.090
But let me take you back to the very first
slides you saw.

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:17.399
We began indexing in 1838, and for almost
135 years, we worked to develop an index of

00:27:17.399 --> 00:27:22.230
the medical literature, so they could be located
and discovered and used by researchers and

00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:23.460
clinicians.

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:29.169
Starting in the 70s, we began to digitize
our indexing tools, so they could be accessed

00:27:29.169 --> 00:27:30.169
and searched.

00:27:30.169 --> 00:27:35.399
And then with the advent of the internet in
1984, we began to look at how our resources

00:27:35.399 --> 00:27:41.171
could be used by individuals around the world,
without this mediation of a specialist, like

00:27:41.171 --> 00:27:42.679
a librarian.

00:27:42.679 --> 00:27:48.450
We face so many similar challenges today with
data, as we faced in the past with managing

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:49.720
the literature.

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:54.950
Making sure that we have access to it, making
sure our investigators know how to use it,

00:27:54.950 --> 00:28:01.260
providing a pathway for people to report,
and present their findings of their research,

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:07.559
so we believe we will be able to manage and
support the due data driven discoveries.

00:28:07.559 --> 00:28:12.950
I'd like to focus on five or six key areas,
that were guiding our plans and our investments

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:14.240
here.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.750
The first is the development and determination
of high value data sets.

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:24.140
High value data sets are data sets that are
worthy of preservation because of their potential

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:26.620
for future discovery from them.

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:31.330
Now high value data set may be a data set
likely to be used by a lot of individuals--

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:36.009
you can think of the Framingham study or the
GTX resource-that's being developed through

00:28:36.009 --> 00:28:40.700
the common fund as types of high value data
sets, or the model organism databases.

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:47.039
But in addition, sometimes data sets are of
high value, because they're unique and not

00:28:47.039 --> 00:28:49.330
able to be replicated.

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:54.139
We have the process of determining high value
data set occurs at multiple times in the research

00:28:54.139 --> 00:28:55.139
process.

00:28:55.139 --> 00:28:58.390
First, of course, at the point of inception
of the original research that creates the

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:03.610
data set, it's worthy to think about what
might the future life of that data set be.

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:05.470
How long will it be useful?

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:06.779
Under what circumstances?

00:29:06.779 --> 00:29:09.750
That will help guide subsequent decisions
about it.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:14.299
Secondly, at the closing of a research process,
which is the point at which many of the curation

00:29:14.299 --> 00:29:18.610
decisions are made, the determination, again,
of which aspects of the data set are high

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:22.919
value to must be preserved, helps to guide
investments in curation.

00:29:22.919 --> 00:29:26.879
And subsequent to that, determining the high
value data set to help us in understanding

00:29:26.879 --> 00:29:33.030
how to ensure that those data sets become
discoverable, knowable, and reachable by investigators

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:35.120
who intend to use them.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:39.919
Essentially, a determination of high value
data sets leads us to being able to preserve

00:29:39.919 --> 00:29:41.259
with a purpose.

00:29:41.259 --> 00:29:46.549
There is no point in preserving every single
data element ever generated in research, but

00:29:46.549 --> 00:29:51.379
we do need to be able to make preservation
decisions that are useful to the community

00:29:51.379 --> 00:29:56.649
of discovery driven research, as well as useful
to the public as a whole.

00:29:56.649 --> 00:30:01.790
So our preservation has to consider, for example,
how likely is a data set to be reused.

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:05.970
Is it going to be used by a small community
of people who are well familiar with the data

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:06.970
set?

00:30:06.970 --> 00:30:11.809
Or is it a larger community that will have
interest in access in that data set?

00:30:11.809 --> 00:30:16.960
We need to use the preservation strategies
to decide the nature of metadata that gets

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:22.460
attached to data sets, what type of storage
resource should it be in, and close and easily

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:27.389
accessible online, or is a tape backup safe
storage we use acceptable for a particular

00:30:27.389 --> 00:30:28.419
data set?

00:30:28.419 --> 00:30:32.509
Finally, the preservation helps us know how
to market the existence and the availability

00:30:32.509 --> 00:30:34.179
of the data sets.

00:30:34.179 --> 00:30:36.490
We talk a lot about metadata.

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:43.370
Metadata being the process of attaching information
to a data set, and to the data elements within

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:44.769
a data set.

00:30:44.769 --> 00:30:50.450
We believe that using standard approaches
to these is the only way to ensure the findablilty

00:30:50.450 --> 00:30:52.080
of the data sets.

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:58.639
And yet, generating standards is a complex
and task that needs to be undertaken in a

00:30:58.639 --> 00:31:00.100
systematic fashion.

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:05.409
We see a lot of interest in the biological
communities, about community driven standards,

00:31:05.409 --> 00:31:07.540
which are a reasonable starting point.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:12.429
But as you see with the list of standards
across the bar in the center of this, the

00:31:12.429 --> 00:31:18.720
kinds of standards we've used in clinical
data management, have been the result of balancing

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:25.549
individual unique communities, like being
a laboratory community being the clinical

00:31:25.549 --> 00:31:27.030
care community.

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:32.409
With the other potential users, participants
in the data use.

00:31:32.409 --> 00:31:38.490
RxNorm, for example, it's a merger of data
standards for medications that draw both from

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:42.660
the pharmaceutical companies, as well as from
the clinical care facilities.

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:47.309
We believe the National Library of Medicine
has a process in place, and a history of developing

00:31:47.309 --> 00:31:51.590
standards, and can service both, as a clearinghouse
and as an integrator of standards.

00:31:51.590 --> 00:31:55.320
We don't make the standards, but we make the
standards accessible.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:59.389
Another aspect that the National Library of
Medicine is, keenly aware that we need to

00:31:59.389 --> 00:32:04.770
invest in across elephant age, is generating
new tools for discovery and analysis.

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:08.620
The methodologies that
we have now, some are very specifically tied

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:13.809
to a specific data set or a specific domain
where the discoveries occurred, we're looking

00:32:13.809 --> 00:32:22.250
to develop both deeply tied and analytical
methods that are unique to a particular areas,

00:32:22.250 --> 00:32:27.870
as well as broad based ones, so we can get
efficiencies across our environments.

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:29.879
We see a great need for promoting training.

00:32:29.879 --> 00:32:34.059
This session is but one of the approaches
to training development, that needs to be

00:32:34.059 --> 00:32:37.140
brought forward to it as is to the society.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:40.570
Training, we see it addressing three different
key groups.

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:43.279
The first, are basic data scientists.

00:32:43.279 --> 00:32:48.220
These are the new algorithm developers, the
new methodological developers.

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:54.280
The second type of training we need, are data
sophisticated investigators, domain specific

00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:58.610
investigators, who have the knowledge and
the insights of how to work with data science

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:03.090
and data scientists to enhance the discoveries
in their area.

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:06.700
And finally, we need data informed front-end
workers.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:12.889
Be they clinicians, or librarians, or even
patients who are able to understand access

00:33:12.889 --> 00:33:17.350
and make use of the products of data driven
science.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:22.879
Data driven science doesn't happen alone,
and it's not only going to happen within the

00:33:22.879 --> 00:33:28.429
NIH funded community, but it needs to be done
in partnership across the federal government,

00:33:28.429 --> 00:33:30.789
across the international borders.

00:33:30.789 --> 00:33:36.250
And we work very closely with the Welcome
Trust with GA4GH, and a number of groups that

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:40.730
are advancing standards and policies around
data sharing.

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:45.390
We recognize that data does not know country
boundaries, does not recognize, in science,

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:47.770
is not held by a single country.

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:51.240
The international efforts that the National
Library of Medicine has been engaged in for

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:56.039
years, will help guide us in the engagement
with our international partners to ensure

00:33:56.039 --> 00:33:59.220
free flowing data and information around the
world.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:03.570
I want to pause for just a minute to remind
you, that the endpoint of data science is

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:06.360
the health of individuals in our society.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:10.990
And whether they be an investigator, such
as the woman on the left, or the clinician,

00:34:10.990 --> 00:34:15.270
the gentleman on the right, or a patient,
we have an ultimate end user.

00:34:15.270 --> 00:34:20.450
And while I don't expect every single scientist,
and every data scientist to have an outreach

00:34:20.450 --> 00:34:24.750
component of their work that ensures that
the everyday layperson understands and benefits

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:29.590
from their work, I do believe it is a responsibility
of the NIH to make sure that our resources

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:35.510
and our discoveries are accessible and known
and useful to an extremely broad range of

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:41.520
individuals, including the very moment of
care at the end user place.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:45.540
Let me close by talking a little bit about
where we're going next to the integration

00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:52.030
with BD2K and the investments in new data
science tools, in the short run, in the next

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:53.639
few years at NIH.

00:34:53.639 --> 00:34:59.010
The BD2K program has been quite a success
developing new methods giving us experience

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:04.520
with testing out, moving of data sets, integration
of data sets, and we will continue to build

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:05.520
with them.

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:11.840
The program, the BD2K program, has moved to
the common fund within our division of program

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:13.820
planning and evaluation.

00:35:13.820 --> 00:35:23.119
And the BD2K program, investigators continue
to meet and are organized to Dr. Betsy Wilder's

00:35:23.119 --> 00:35:25.540
office there, in the common fund.

00:35:25.540 --> 00:35:31.290
The activities that the BD2K program is supporting,
right at this time-- from January of '17 through

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:37.589
August of '18-is to synthesize and collect
learnings from the centers of excellence,

00:35:37.589 --> 00:35:41.430
of which there were 12, to conduct the
commons pilots.

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:45.960
Now this program name, in the last two weeks,
has changed to The Vanguard.

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:51.099
Within The Vanguard, what was originally known
as the commons pilots, three high value data

00:35:51.099 --> 00:36:00.220
sets, TopMed, GTEX, and the MODS, have been
identified, and will be prepared for storage

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:04.130
in a common structure that will be a cloud
based structure.

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:09.710
The plan is to establish a foundation for
authentication, authorization, and curation

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:15.550
that is scalable, but specifically gives us
experience with taking high value data sets

00:36:15.550 --> 00:36:18.210
and moving them into a cloud environment.

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:26.339
There will be much attention given to the
cost of managing data and moving data to a

00:36:26.339 --> 00:36:32.099
cloud environment to make it more accessible,
make it more re-usable, and interoperable.

00:36:32.099 --> 00:36:37.050
And also the experience of investigators,
and their investigator teams, at the institutions

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:42.930
both in terms of data deposit, as well as
in terms of actually using an operating on

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:44.370
the data.

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:52.819
We expect also to be stimulating new inquiry
in this area, and releasing at least one funding

00:36:52.819 --> 00:36:59.250
announcement to specifically conduct analysis
on two or more of these high value data sets,

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:01.740
TopMed, GTEX, for example, or the MODS.

00:37:01.740 --> 00:37:09.660
There will be a new funding opportunity released
this summer before the end of fiscal '17,

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:13.670
which will be done under our other transactions
authority.

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:18.589
So it won't be a research based grant, but
we're looking for partnerships to develop

00:37:18.589 --> 00:37:24.190
the authentication and authorization strategies
to establish a curation approach, and also

00:37:24.190 --> 00:37:30.839
to help with the logistics of making transitions
to cloud environments feasible for more people.

00:37:30.839 --> 00:37:35.630
There continues to be investment in training
our BD2K T32 programs, are continuing.

00:37:35.630 --> 00:37:39.910
And as I indicated earlier, there is an investment
in curation.

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:47.400
All of these have been currently funded, are
extensions into new programs awaits fiscal

00:37:47.400 --> 00:37:51.290
'18, when I expect to see new funding announcements
come forward.

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:56.210
We are though, emphasizing our development
of the infrastructure on the NIH campus.

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:58.839
In front of you, is a picture of most of the
campus.

00:37:58.839 --> 00:38:02.070
One of our big buildings to the right is missing.

00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:06.921
That funny, diamond shaped building, with
the diamond shaped top, that's the National

00:38:06.921 --> 00:38:07.921
Library of Medicine.

00:38:07.921 --> 00:38:09.860
We're right at the front edge of the campus.

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:14.130
We will be building the commons framework,
as is depicted here.

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:19.640
And this is from Warren Kibbe and Vivien Bonazzi's
work to get you to understand, that what we

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:24.270
believe we're building at NIH is a compute
platform, that will be managed through commercial

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:25.690
cloud services.

00:38:25.690 --> 00:38:31.990
We will be putting in place [INAUDIBLE] containers
for creating packaging of applications and

00:38:31.990 --> 00:38:36.150
developing a digital object identifier process.

00:38:36.150 --> 00:38:39.180
Test it out first with these three high value
data sets.

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:43.839
We are going to be integrating some of the
APIs that were developed under the BD2K program,

00:38:43.839 --> 00:38:48.820
and developing new ones, and also enhancing
the search process and search for discoverability

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:50.750
and findability.

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:56.800
At the National Library of Medicine, we are
also involved in moving data science into

00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:59.280
our portfolio.

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:05.330
We are in the process of partnering with groups
to work on creating the mechanisms to determine

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:11.010
high value data sets, and for creating
specifically forecasting cost and utilization

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:13.750
is the emphasis in the program we're doing
here.

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:18.790
We will be implementing efficient and secure
preservation strategies, to facilitate access

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:20.160
and reuse.

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:26.970
Beginning in December of this year, we will
be opening the PubMed Central to allow data

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:31.470
deposit with articles, as they are entered
into PubMed.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:36.640
That will be our first step at opening a secure
preservation strategy that makes data accessible

00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:38.680
through individual articles.

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:45.079
We will be continuing to stimulate intramural
efforts and standards development, and try

00:39:45.079 --> 00:39:50.210
to bring some order of organization to the
standards processes.

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:53.750
Developing new methods for data management
and data driven discovery, our methodological

00:39:53.750 --> 00:40:00.690
discoveries are emphasizing in the natural
language processing area, and in machine learning

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:02.600
specifically, for the short grow.

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:05.619
Our workforce investment continues.

00:40:05.619 --> 00:40:10.080
Many of our NLM training programs now have
added a data science dimension.

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:14.790
We anticipate opening a new data management
training program, in the future.

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:23.380
Now in the T32, and perhaps the F31 mechanism,
we continue to engage across the government,

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:28.349
particularly with NSF, and with NOA to look
at safe and efficient strategies for data

00:40:28.349 --> 00:40:30.309
management and storage.

00:40:30.309 --> 00:40:35.300
And hope to be expanding this, and will be
expanding this, through collaborations with

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:36.330
others.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:41.970
Finally, we are doing all of this under a
concept of fostering of the idea of open science,

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:48.551
of making science accessible to the community
to ensure that the process from discovery,

00:40:48.551 --> 00:40:53.750
from an initial inception of what research
ideas are interesting to pursue, to what research

00:40:53.750 --> 00:41:00.170
should be pursued, what funding should be
done to actually interpreting and disseminating

00:41:00.170 --> 00:41:05.670
information, being done in a way that actually
brings more openness engaging industry, engaging

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:12.420
science, society, and also engaging the individuals,
who have the ability to lead in a scientific

00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:14.660
workforce development.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:20.819
Finally, we are absolutely committed to making
sure that the findings from the BD2K program

00:41:20.819 --> 00:41:25.430
are in the Cloud Pilots, The Vangard program
now, are made available and accessible to

00:41:25.430 --> 00:41:28.460
as broader a community as possible.

00:41:28.460 --> 00:41:34.950
A key aspect of this, for our work here, is
going to be the establishment of a structure

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:40.420
for the development of data science infrastructure
at NIH.

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:46.599
And I deliberately selected this image of
this series of blocks, because what I envision

00:41:46.599 --> 00:41:50.869
in the future, is we will have a highly distributed
data structure.

00:41:50.869 --> 00:41:56.540
Our data commons at NIH will be but one of
the commons where data will be residing in

00:41:56.540 --> 00:41:57.609
the future.

00:41:57.609 --> 00:42:02.240
And our goals for developing this, and our
goals for making sure data are accessible

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:08.450
and knowable across a large community, require
us to have in place, a stable structure that

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:15.650
guides the acquisition in development and
distribution of data, as it goes forward.

00:42:15.650 --> 00:42:20.349
I envision there will be a division with the
National Library of Medicine, that focuses

00:42:20.349 --> 00:42:25.690
specifically on data science, and it will
partner with our science investigators around

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:29.930
the NIH, both in the intramural and the extramural
programs.

00:42:29.930 --> 00:42:34.610
I also envision that their resources, that
are already in place in the National Library

00:42:34.610 --> 00:42:37.230
of Medicine, will help us reach the public
better.

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:41.960
So our community of the national networks
of libraries of medicine, which provide us

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:50.349
with 6500 points of presence around the country,
will have, in place, libraries and library

00:42:50.349 --> 00:42:54.520
resources that can
assist institutions, and universities, and

00:42:54.520 --> 00:42:57.940
investigators, to develop effective methods
for working with data.

00:42:57.940 --> 00:43:01.480
Increasingly, in our academic health science
centers, we're seeing that our librarians

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:07.380
are taking a key role in helping investigators
prepare their data for deposit, and also to

00:43:07.380 --> 00:43:13.320
identify, and locate, and manage large data
sets, much the same way that we did the literature.

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:16.950
So the librarians aren't going to do the data
science, the same way the librarians didn't

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:21.780
do the basic science that created the articles
that grew out of investigations, but instead,

00:43:21.780 --> 00:43:27.369
they're going to provide the facilitation,
the information infrastructure, for science.

00:43:27.369 --> 00:43:30.720
We believe, also through the nationalized
network of libraries of medicine, that we

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:35.220
will be able to help the general public better
understand where our data driven discoveries

00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:36.309
affect their lives.

00:43:36.309 --> 00:43:42.940
For example, participants in All Of Us program,
which intends to obtain biological samples,

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:49.230
as well as other types of assessments from
over 1 million people, will be best served

00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:52.670
if there's a library in the community that
can help people understand what it means when

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:58.470
one gets an individual test result back, or
an individual genetic profile back, through

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:00.290
the All Of Us program.

00:44:00.290 --> 00:44:05.349
Having trusted information resources that
are able to support the data driven enterprise,

00:44:05.349 --> 00:44:11.020
is both the end point of the data driven discovery
process, as well as a service that we believe

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:13.299
the government can provide.

00:44:13.299 --> 00:44:18.660
I will leave you now with just one announcement
that you may have heard before, but an important

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:22.260
leader in the data science initiative at NIH
will be leading us.

00:44:22.260 --> 00:44:27.170
Dr. David Lipman, the founding director of
the NCBI, is going to be stepping down next

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:30.359
week after almost 30 years of service to NIH.

00:44:30.359 --> 00:44:35.280
He greatly improved their access to biomedical
information and genomic information.

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:40.099
His discoveries of blasts, his standing up
in the dbGaP, that is critical, important

00:44:40.099 --> 00:44:44.360
offerings from the National Library of Medicine
and the NIH.

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:46.329
Dr. Litmus is making a major career change.

00:44:46.329 --> 00:44:50.920
He's going to be assuming the position of
the chief science officer at Impossible Foods,

00:44:50.920 --> 00:44:55.670
which is a new company in California that's
applying molecular biology to the food industry

00:44:55.670 --> 00:44:58.430
to create vegan based foods.

00:44:58.430 --> 00:45:02.640
We wish Dr. Lipman his great success, but
recognize that many of the comments that I

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:07.910
made today, and mention the vision of data
science at NIH, was shaped by this brilliant

00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:10.840
colleague, and I am grateful for his work.

00:45:10.840 --> 00:45:14.990
I'll close by-- oh, got one more picture him,
sorry.

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:19.160
Dr. Lipman was around at the very beginning
of the development of the NCBI.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:27.650
You see him here with Claude Pepper and Dr.
[INAUDIBLE], as they were discussing the launch

00:45:27.650 --> 00:45:33.020
of NCBI, before it became what it is today,
which was a great and incredible resource

00:45:33.020 --> 00:45:34.349
for data.

00:45:34.349 --> 00:45:38.119
And I close by now showing you that you can
reach me in many ways.

00:45:38.119 --> 00:45:43.420
Through a blog called Musings From The Mezzanine,
which focuses on the National Library of Medicine

00:45:43.420 --> 00:45:44.420
resources.

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:49.770
Another blog called Data Science at NIH, which
focuses on our data science activities here.

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:51.770
I'm on Twitter and pretty active.

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:53.510
And I'm also available by email.

00:45:53.510 --> 00:45:58.900
I now hope I've given us enough time to have
some conversation about the directions for

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:02.010
the future, and I welcome your thoughts and
your ideas.

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:03.060
Thanks very much.

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:10.440
Dr. Brennan, thank you so much for that wonderful
overview of data science from the NIH.

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:16.220
It seems very comprehensive, and a lot of
thought seems to have gone into what the future

00:46:16.220 --> 00:46:19.020
of data science is going to be at the NIH.

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:25.030
Moving forward, there are a number of questions,
as you might imagine, from a comprehensive

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:26.109
talk like yours.

00:46:26.109 --> 00:46:31.490
I probably can't get to all of them, but per
your request, I will try and copy all of them,

00:46:31.490 --> 00:46:35.550
and I'll forward them to you, so that you
can kind of get a sense of the things that

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:37.089
people are curious about.

00:46:37.089 --> 00:46:40.040
But I'll sample a few and maybe you can address
them.

00:46:40.040 --> 00:46:45.770
I know that there was a lot of discussion
about the commons and about the role that

00:46:45.770 --> 00:46:51.380
that's going to play, and there were some
questions about the role of existing kind

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:55.819
of data sharing efforts that are being done
through the NIH.

00:46:55.819 --> 00:47:00.559
For example, like the National Cancer Institute's
genomics data commons, I know there's some

00:47:00.559 --> 00:47:06.140
work that was spearheaded, formerly by Dr.
Insel at the NIMH, for example the Autism

00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:11.540
Centers of Excellence, where you're expected
to provide your data as a condition of your

00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:15.950
funding to the national database of autism
research, and other programs.

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:18.069
How will those play a role?

00:47:18.069 --> 00:47:19.069
Will they benefit?

00:47:19.069 --> 00:47:21.310
Will they become partners in the new data
commons?

00:47:21.310 --> 00:47:23.180
How do you see that working?

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:32.790
Well I'm going to take you back to my-- I
hope I'm going to take you back to my slide--

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:38.560
to show you that the structure that we envision
is, if you will, a highly distributed structure.

00:47:38.560 --> 00:47:44.849
So the NIH data commons will be but one of
what we envision as a whole network of commons,

00:47:44.849 --> 00:47:48.400
as in data repositories, around the world.

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:54.310
And are our challenges in creating proper
discovery mechanisms, catalogs, for example,

00:47:54.310 --> 00:47:58.440
it's not dissimilar to the challenge that
was originally faced when the World Wide Web

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:01.660
and the domain name servers had to come about.

00:48:01.660 --> 00:48:08.619
So we're at the beginning of needing to be
able to locate and share at the level of repositories,

00:48:08.619 --> 00:48:11.069
and then at the level of data sets.

00:48:11.069 --> 00:48:16.280
We envision that-- we worked very closely
with Warren Kibbe and the Cancer Genome commons,

00:48:16.280 --> 00:48:22.180
and also with the Brain Initiative, with the
Autism commons-- and we do not see those as

00:48:22.180 --> 00:48:28.410
being, in any way, closed by this process,
by creating an NIH data commons.

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:34.710
But rather, the NIH data commons is yet another
participant in this activity of data sharing.

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:40.500
Now I will say, that at this point in time,
all of the focus in developing the NIH commons

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:44.299
is targeted around known data sets.

00:48:44.299 --> 00:48:50.099
And we see going forward, that the models
for curating known data sets might be slightly

00:48:50.099 --> 00:48:52.710
different than the models for creating new
data sets.

00:48:52.710 --> 00:48:59.680
And again, we're working with a trans NIH
group, to make sure that we first of all,

00:48:59.680 --> 00:49:05.359
don't tie ourselves to an our archiving model,
that actually isn't as robust as the future

00:49:05.359 --> 00:49:09.450
we'll need, but second, so that we don't lose
our connections to those models.

00:49:09.450 --> 00:49:15.840
Let me say, just briefly, we expect that we
will be connecting, the data commons infrastructure,

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:17.270
will connect to data commons.

00:49:17.270 --> 00:49:23.400
Are there other data repositories held in
different places, including within universities,

00:49:23.400 --> 00:49:27.260
including at research institutes, and in other
countries.

00:49:27.260 --> 00:49:33.319
The negotiation of what constitutes access,
exchange, what kinds of compute resources

00:49:33.319 --> 00:49:40.390
are going to be available, will, I believe,
make for a of substantial amount of conversation,

00:49:40.390 --> 00:49:43.720
as well as indexing of resources over the
next few

00:49:43.720 --> 00:49:44.720
years.

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:47.420
We're in an early stage of this.

00:49:47.420 --> 00:49:51.100
We fundamentally believe the distributed structure
is essential.

00:49:51.100 --> 00:49:54.900
When one makes a commitment to a distributed
structure, one makes a commitment to invest

00:49:54.900 --> 00:49:57.809
in discovery tools, as much as analytic tools.

00:49:57.809 --> 00:50:02.460
That was another kind of series of questions
had to do with.

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:08.799
The role of tools, even though the commons
is being constructed now, at the NIH itself,

00:50:08.799 --> 00:50:14.619
do you envision future opportunities for people
who develop tools, which leverage the commons

00:50:14.619 --> 00:50:20.490
and the data in it and it's metadata, as part
of some of your new funding initiatives that

00:50:20.490 --> 00:50:22.680
you see on the horizon.

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:23.680
Absolutely.

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:31.230
I'm quite committed to accelerating methods
developments, which is a necessary precursor

00:50:31.230 --> 00:50:34.799
to accelerating knowledge development.

00:50:34.799 --> 00:50:40.830
We are considering, for example, what does
a library of models look like?

00:50:40.830 --> 00:50:47.060
How do we curate models the way we curate
the literature, so an executable version of

00:50:47.060 --> 00:50:51.820
a model becomes accessible and knowable?

00:50:51.820 --> 00:50:56.400
So we anticipate that we're going to have
to invest in three areas, with respect to

00:50:56.400 --> 00:50:57.400
models.

00:50:57.400 --> 00:50:58.920
First, of course, model development.

00:50:58.920 --> 00:51:05.650
And we work with the intergovernment modeling
analysis and simulation group, on the beginning

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:07.349
parts of the step.

00:51:07.349 --> 00:51:12.240
But in addition, we know we need to find a
way to distribute models more effectively

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:17.220
than in the text narrative of an article,
or as an appendage to an article.

00:51:17.220 --> 00:51:25.349
So we are looking to find a mechanism for
constructing model libraries, discoverable

00:51:25.349 --> 00:51:26.349
miles of libraries.

00:51:26.349 --> 00:51:33.240
And finally, we need to find ways where we
can efficiently link models and data sets,

00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:42.260
in a way that allows them to be interrogated,
but isn't terribly redundant, or complex in

00:51:42.260 --> 00:51:43.260
its organization.

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:48.850
And by that, I mean, that as a model is modified
for a particular data set, it is really important

00:51:48.850 --> 00:51:53.549
to be able to recover that exact instance
of the model with that data set, if one wants

00:51:53.549 --> 00:51:56.250
to try to reproduce a set of findings.

00:51:56.250 --> 00:52:02.150
And the way we do this in the literature right
now, is we have a citation database, we have

00:52:02.150 --> 00:52:07.280
investigators describe their background or
place their working in a certain scientific

00:52:07.280 --> 00:52:14.049
tradition, and then use text to describe the
procedures they will be doing to carry out

00:52:14.049 --> 00:52:17.000
an experiment, or that they did use to carry
out an experiment.

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:20.869
As we move into data driven discovery, we
need to be thinking about different ways of

00:52:20.869 --> 00:52:26.420
documenting the scientific process, as well
as archiving that documentation.

00:52:26.420 --> 00:52:31.690
So I see a whole range of new scientific communication
tools emerging, along with the discovery tools,

00:52:31.690 --> 00:52:33.060
and the analytical tools.

00:52:33.060 --> 00:52:36.560
It's very interesting that you mentioned libraries.

00:52:36.560 --> 00:52:41.960
One of the things that, at least I have learned
through our running of these data science

00:52:41.960 --> 00:52:47.910
seminar series, is the interest and potential
source of energy, if you will, that comes

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:52.380
from librarians and library scientists, and
their potential role here.

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:57.799
And we had a question from someone asking,
will the National Library of Medicine be hiring

00:52:57.799 --> 00:53:04.609
additional librarians who have data science
experience, following a trend across the country

00:53:04.609 --> 00:53:05.720
apparently.

00:53:05.720 --> 00:53:07.299
This is a great question.

00:53:07.299 --> 00:53:11.329
It would surprise-- maybe not everyone, but
certainly surprised me when I first came here

00:53:11.329 --> 00:53:15.570
last summer-- that we have 1700 men and women
that work here at the National Library of

00:53:15.570 --> 00:53:19.580
Medicine, and we only have 97 librarians.

00:53:19.580 --> 00:53:24.720
Which tells you that the work of a library
takes a lot of different disciplines together,

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:26.580
the least of which is librarians.

00:53:26.580 --> 00:53:34.270
We do anticipate that our library operations
group, which manages the collection that we

00:53:34.270 --> 00:53:41.750
have, that is, what do we physically own,
as well as the curation our Medline literature

00:53:41.750 --> 00:53:50.410
citation process, will be enriched with individuals
who will be helping with the data curation

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:51.410
process.

00:53:51.410 --> 00:53:56.510
What we expect to have happen, and I anticipated
the development of something that might look

00:53:56.510 --> 00:53:58.150
like a PubMed for data.

00:53:58.150 --> 00:54:02.840
Now people here don't like to say it quite
that way, because it's not a direct equivalent,

00:54:02.840 --> 00:54:10.270
but just as we have a archive tagging system,
JATS system, we are in the process of working

00:54:10.270 --> 00:54:17.480
with the biocaddie group to refine the DATS,
D-A-T-S, which is a data archiving tagging

00:54:17.480 --> 00:54:18.480
system.

00:54:18.480 --> 00:54:26.309
So we'll be able to put a high level metadata
structure that enable us to catalog data.

00:54:26.309 --> 00:54:32.500
That will require that we use the library
science skills, and cataloging, and creating,

00:54:32.500 --> 00:54:36.450
and indexing, so individuals can find the
data sets.

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:43.500
And just as we don't hold every article that
is listed in Medline we would not hold the

00:54:43.500 --> 00:54:47.819
owner of every data set that is listed in
a data catalog, but rather provide the pathway

00:54:47.819 --> 00:54:48.980
to it.

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:53.270
I see librarians as having two other really
key roles.

00:54:53.270 --> 00:54:58.510
Librarians, if brought into a research projects
from the very beginning, can help the research

00:54:58.510 --> 00:55:03.630
team describe the data management plan, in
a way that addresses sustainability.

00:55:03.630 --> 00:55:11.830
And ensures that the metadata, even early
curation can occur, as data is being acquired,

00:55:11.830 --> 00:55:14.530
not simply waiting until the data set is complete.

00:55:14.530 --> 00:55:18.440
And the second aspect is that our library
science community is going to be of great

00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:20.340
assistance to our investigators.

00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:25.539
And frankly, to the public in general-- small
businesses, industry-- in actually locating

00:55:25.539 --> 00:55:26.539
data sets.

00:55:26.539 --> 00:55:33.510
Because the process of searching for data
sets and locating them is not nearly as refined

00:55:33.510 --> 00:55:35.460
as the process of searching for the literature.

00:55:35.460 --> 00:55:39.710
So we'll need all the library science talent
we can get.

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:43.880
That's probably going to be good news to a
number of people.

00:55:43.880 --> 00:55:46.770
You mentioned data sharing plans.

00:55:46.770 --> 00:55:52.580
And I know that the NIH has been very interested
in increasing the description of how people

00:55:52.580 --> 00:55:56.530
will be sharing their data across a variety
of grants, typically these have been at the

00:55:56.530 --> 00:56:02.390
more expensive end of the grant spectrum,
if you will, but this is increasingly suspected

00:56:02.390 --> 00:56:07.700
that it's going to be coming down to R01s
and R21s, et cetera.

00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:14.900
How does the commons play into that, or inform,
that policy of what could be shared, and when

00:56:14.900 --> 00:56:19.000
it can be shared, under the grant mechanism
framework?

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:20.970
So this is a really good question.

00:56:20.970 --> 00:56:28.069
And some of you may be familiar with the rather
strong separation between intramural and extramural

00:56:28.069 --> 00:56:32.580
research at the NIH, but in the data science,
particularly in the data sharing process,

00:56:32.580 --> 00:56:36.839
those two areas get blended quite quickly.

00:56:36.839 --> 00:56:46.470
We envision that publicly funded research,
and the data generated through publicly funded

00:56:46.470 --> 00:56:48.490
research, should be available to the public.

00:56:48.490 --> 00:56:50.380
That's an open science framework.

00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:53.640
So having
said that, we need to find ways to help investigators,

00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:57.690
in an efficient way, be able to make their
data sharable.

00:56:57.690 --> 00:57:00.790
And that is the basis underlying the idea
of the commons.

00:57:00.790 --> 00:57:03.810
There will be a safe place for the data to
reside.

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:10.109
Now if we start thinking about the 18,000
NIH funded projects, that are going on on

00:57:10.109 --> 00:57:14.809
this very day, that's a lot of different data
sets to archive, and to think that they will

00:57:14.809 --> 00:57:15.950
continue to be growing.

00:57:15.950 --> 00:57:20.869
So we would expect that there will be-- not
so much from the commons, but through our

00:57:20.869 --> 00:57:26.630
office of science policy, and through our
materials in the funding announcements, and

00:57:26.630 --> 00:57:37.049
in the guides-- there will be guidance, regarding
how to prepare a data set for a deposit.

00:57:37.049 --> 00:57:40.240
How to determine which parts of the data set
need to be shared.

00:57:40.240 --> 00:57:48.799
How to identify an acceptable storage opportunity
for a data set, because we don't envision

00:57:48.799 --> 00:57:55.309
ever requiring that all data generated in
all NIH projects, be only stored at the NIH,

00:57:55.309 --> 00:58:01.150
but rather that we foster the use of appropriate
types of archiving for the data, and appropriate

00:58:01.150 --> 00:58:02.530
types of discoverability.

00:58:02.530 --> 00:58:08.520
I must comment, for just a moment, on the
idea of what we call deaccessioning data sets.

00:58:08.520 --> 00:58:14.100
When a data set is no longer actively used,
or actively in need, we need to have policies

00:58:14.100 --> 00:58:16.680
in place that describe what happens next.

00:58:16.680 --> 00:58:19.300
Does it go to a lower, long term storage environment?

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:21.799
Does it remain in an index?

00:58:21.799 --> 00:58:27.410
Do we need to retain all of the modeling that
was done with the data set to ensure that

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:29.050
it can continue to be used?

00:58:29.050 --> 00:58:34.559
Those questions, now, are going to require
extensive comment from the community, as well

00:58:34.559 --> 00:58:37.710
as propose as plans developed here at the
NIH.

00:58:37.710 --> 00:58:39.710
That's great.

00:58:39.710 --> 00:58:44.760
One question-- this is sort of a two pronged
question, if you'll indulge me for a second--

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:49.710
but we've got a number of questions about
this, so I'm trying to synthesize.

00:58:49.710 --> 00:58:54.340
I always think you mention the fair principles,
and we don't need to go into what they all

00:58:54.340 --> 00:59:00.390
mean, but I've always kind of secretly felt
that there is a silent "E" on the end of fair,

00:59:00.390 --> 00:59:05.150
that you don't hear, but that stands for education
and training.

00:59:05.150 --> 00:59:09.310
And that the role of education and training
in data science is absolutely fundamental

00:59:09.310 --> 00:59:17.029
to, not only helping people who are not biomedical
scientists, but from statisticians and computer

00:59:17.029 --> 00:59:21.990
scientists get into looking at these very
rich sources of data.

00:59:21.990 --> 00:59:26.080
I want to hear a little bit more about what
you feel the role of education will be, but

00:59:26.080 --> 00:59:30.369
also how that touches on the role of trust,
which you also mention, in terms of public

00:59:30.369 --> 00:59:31.369
outreach.

00:59:31.369 --> 00:59:35.690
I think now we're about two or three weeks
after the March for Science, and the notion

00:59:35.690 --> 00:59:42.210
of trustworthiness and science and maybe some
distrust from certain portions of the American

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:43.369
population.

00:59:43.369 --> 00:59:46.309
I think that's an important role, and I want
to hear what your thoughts are on that.

00:59:46.309 --> 00:59:49.440
Well you don't become a data scientist at
the PhD level.

00:59:49.440 --> 00:59:54.890
You have to begin thinking about data science
from the K12 experience.

00:59:54.890 --> 01:00:00.029
So we are partnering with the NSF to look
at how we can influence and strengthening

01:00:00.029 --> 01:00:04.180
the foundations for data science in the K-12
education program.

01:00:04.180 --> 01:00:09.859
Children who are exposed to probability theory,
and fourth graders, and nuances about algebra,

01:00:09.859 --> 01:00:13.440
by the time they're in the sixth grade, will
do much better in the future.

01:00:13.440 --> 01:00:21.740
And we will be able to concentrate our university
based data science training building on the

01:00:21.740 --> 01:00:26.180
substrate of knowledge that individuals bring
at that moment.

01:00:26.180 --> 01:00:31.559
We also have to think about the generations
of investigators, and analysts, and librarians,

01:00:31.559 --> 01:00:36.450
and clinicians who are in their 30s, 40s,
and 50s, who grew up with experimental models

01:00:36.450 --> 01:00:41.740
of knowledge building and evidence generation,
and need to move over into complement that--

01:00:41.740 --> 01:00:44.990
not replace it completely-- but complement
it with data science.

01:00:44.990 --> 01:00:50.660
So I would envision that we will have our
future training, NIH funded training, will

01:00:50.660 --> 01:00:55.980
include early career, mid-career opportunities
for investigators to get deeper training in

01:00:55.980 --> 01:00:57.349
the data sciences.

01:00:57.349 --> 01:01:01.910
We expect to be partnering with the Medical
Library Association, and with our library

01:01:01.910 --> 01:01:07.660
colleagues through the Association of Research
Libraries, to build effective, in the moment

01:01:07.660 --> 01:01:13.579
training for our workers, who need to have
access to understanding about data science

01:01:13.579 --> 01:01:17.000
and where it fits into the process of inquiry,
but don't have the ability to leave to get

01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:19.130
another degree.

01:01:19.130 --> 01:01:22.450
That's great.

01:01:22.450 --> 01:01:28.000
Did you have any final thoughts on public
outreach and how the data science commons

01:01:28.000 --> 01:01:30.109
might help with that?

01:01:30.109 --> 01:01:38.359
Well, I believe that our ability to be transparent--
and I'm demonstrating why scientific conclusions

01:01:38.359 --> 01:01:43.580
arose and the pathways from which they arose--
will enhance the public trust.

01:01:43.580 --> 01:01:47.960
I believe that at the National Library of
Medicine, in particular, and the NIH in general,

01:01:47.960 --> 01:01:50.030
enjoys a great deal of the public trust.

01:01:50.030 --> 01:01:54.890
And so the strategies that we take to make
data accessible, not only through the scientific

01:01:54.890 --> 01:02:00.349
channels, but also through public libraries,
is an important part of how we will be engendering

01:02:00.349 --> 01:02:01.710
trust from the public.

01:02:01.710 --> 01:02:08.520
At the same time, we have to help the public
develop numeracy and an understanding of probabilistic

01:02:08.520 --> 01:02:15.060
and thinking, because-- not at the same depth
of an investigator-- but we need to move from

01:02:15.060 --> 01:02:23.039
a society that largely views points as truisms,
to understanding ranges and pathways and patterns,

01:02:23.039 --> 01:02:30.010
so that we don't oversell the result that
a particular study or a particular investigation

01:02:30.010 --> 01:02:31.010
provides.

01:02:31.010 --> 01:02:38.000
And again, the idea of building a quantitatively
sophisticated public, will require us engaging

01:02:38.000 --> 01:02:41.050
at the very earliest levels.

01:02:41.050 --> 01:02:45.630
Well with that, I'd like to thank you so much,
Dr. Brennan, for that wonderful overview of

01:02:45.630 --> 01:02:48.500
NIH data science thinking.

01:02:48.500 --> 01:02:53.220
The pathway forward and some of the details
about how you envision that happening.

01:02:53.220 --> 01:02:54.430
I think this was outstanding.

01:02:54.430 --> 01:03:00.950
In an outstanding way to end our series of
data science seminars, funded through the

01:03:00.950 --> 01:03:04.599
BD2K program, which have been just so wonderfully
attended.

01:03:04.599 --> 01:03:09.680
And so I want to thank all of our attendees
for their participation, and any feedback

01:03:09.680 --> 01:03:14.019
that you have will be welcomed to receive
that.

01:03:14.019 --> 01:03:15.839
So once again, thank you so much.

01:03:15.839 --> 01:03:17.269
And thank you, Dr. Brennan.

01:03:17.269 --> 01:03:18.269
Thank you, John.

01:03:18.269 --> 01:03:23.220
And I'll comment on some of the items in my
blog, if it's possible to do so, so keep watching.

01:03:23.220 --> 01:03:24.220
Thank you.

01:03:24.220 --> 01:03:25.670
Thank you so much.

01:03:25.670 --> 01:03:29.109
Thank you, everybody.

